    {"problem": "The AC runs all afternoon and the upstairs never drops below 26°C", "solution": "In the big two-storey homes in Barrhaven and Stittsville, the second floor carries the attic heat and the afternoon sun. The unit may be low on charge, the coil may be dirty, or the ducts may be starving the upstairs. We check the refrigerant circuit, measure the temperature drop across the coil and look at the returns before we blame the equipment."},
    {"problem": "The outdoor unit won't start after sitting under snow all winter", "solution": "Five months under a drift is hard on an air conditioner. We find corroded contactors, swollen capacitors, bent fan blades and wiring chewed by mice looking for a warm spot. Most of it is a one-visit repair once we've tested each part."},
    {"problem": "The air is cool but the house feels clammy", "solution": "Ottawa's summer humidity is the real enemy. An oversized or single-stage AC hits the setpoint fast and shuts off before it has pulled the moisture out. We check airflow and charge first. If the unit is simply too big for the house, we'll say so and show you what a right-sized inverter system does differently."},
    {"problem": "Ice on the copper line or the indoor coil by mid-afternoon", "solution": "Ice means low airflow or low refrigerant. A clogged filter, a matted evaporator coil or a slow leak will do it. We thaw the system, find the cause and fix it, because running a frozen AC can flood the furnace and kill the compressor."},
